 after completing <1>physical therapy</1>.     4. <2>Left buttock and anterior thigh pain</2>, stable and likely due to <3>OA</3>. Recc <4>heat</4>, <5>massage</5>, and <6>ibuprofen</6>. This improved after completing <7>physical therapy</7>.     5. <8>Osteoporosis</8>, currently on *****. The patient's baseline <9>bone density scan</9> showed <10>osteoporosis</10> in the low back. She had a repeat <11>bone density scan</11> last July 2018 which showed slight improvement of bone density at the femur neck with <12>T-score</12> (-2.4) now in the <13>osteopenia</13> range. The <14>T-scores</14> of her lumbar spine and femur are stable from last year which is in the <15>osteopenia</15> range. Advised patient to continue <16>calcium</16> and <17>Vitamin D supplements</17> and to continue weight bearing exercises. ***** continue monitor <18>DEXA scan</18>. If the patient's <19>BMD</19> falls below -2.5, she will need to see a dentist, then we could start her on <20>Prolia 60 mg SQ every 6 months</20>. We can check again her <21>DEXA</21> in 1 year to assess change, due July 2019.     6. <22>Headaches</22>, intermittent, chronic: Seek care if worsening. Advised to follow-up with <23>PCP</23>. If worsening, consider <24>brain MRI</24>.     7. <25>Loose stools</25>, grade 1, mostly after meals. Discussed trying daily <26>probiotics</26>.        Follow up in 6 months or sooner if she has new or worsening symptoms.     I spent a total of 40 minutes face-to-face with the patient and 30 minutes of that time was spent counseling regarding the treatment plan, symptoms and therapeutic options.         
