ner_results: ```plaintext
enhancement along the <1>sacral foramina</1> and <2>sacral canal</2>. <3>T12-L1</3>: Areas mild epidural <4>tumor</4> at <5>T12</5> compressing the <6>thecal sac</6> ventrally. <7>L1-L2</7>: Mild <8>facet hypertrophy</8> and mild <9>central stenosis</9>. <10>L2-L3</10>: Mild <11>dural thickening</11> and <12>facet hypertrophy</12>. <13>L3-L4</13>: Is asymmetrical epidural fat compressing the <14>thecal sac</14> with moderate <15>stenosis</15>. It is mild <16>dural enhancement</16>. There is mild <17>tumor enhancement</17> posterior to the <18>psoas muscle</18> on the right side at <19>L4</19>. Measures approximately <20>12 mm</20>. <21>L4-L5</21>: <22>Disc bulge</22> and right sided small <23>disc herniation</23> resulting in right lateral recess and <24>neural foraminal stenosis</24>. Mild <25>dural contrast enhancement</25>. <26>L5-S1</26>: There is no central <27>skull tumor</27>. There is <28>epidural thickening</28>. This is extending from <29>S1 segment</29>.  <30>02/10/17 MRI Thoracic spine w/ and w/o contrast</30>: <31>Thoracic metastases</31> throughout the <32>spine</32>. Minimal compression of <33>T6</33>. There is <34>spinal CORD compression</34> due to epidural <35>tumor</35> and thickening from approximately <36>T5-T9</36>. There is separate mild epidural <37>tumor</37> and <38>thecal sac</38> and <39>cord deformity</39> at <40>T12</40>. The <41>epidural enhancement</41> is contiguous throughout the <42>thoracic spine</42>.  <43>02/10/17 MRI Cervical spine w/ and w/o contrast</43>: Heterogeneous <44>marrow signal</44> throughout the <45>cervical spine</45> consistent with <46>metastatic replacement</46>. No epidural <47>tumor</47>. No intraspinal <48>meningeal contrast enhancement</48>. <49>C5-C6</49>: Mild <50>bulge</50> with mild <51>central stenosis</51>.  <52>02/10/17 Bone scan</52>: Multiple focal areas of increased activity are visualized in the <53>skull</53>, <54>cervical</54>, <55>thoracic</55> and <56>lumbar spine</56>, multiple <57>ribs</57> bilaterally <58>sternum</58>, <59>pelvis</59>, proximal <60>humeri</60>, <61>hips</61> and <62>femurs</62>. Findings correlates with <63>lesions</63> seen on <64>MRI</64> and are consistent with widespread <65>metastatic involvement</65>. Of incidental note, there is no evidence of mass abnormality of the <66>kidneys</66> or obstructive <67>uropathy</67>.   <68>02/10/17 MRI Brain</68>: Small <69>dural based metastases</69> are present along the posterior <70>interhemispheric falx</70> and right <71>parietal convexity</71>. These measure approximately <72>1 cm</72> or less.
``` 
