<1>biopsy</1> was performed. Imaging finding and <2>malignant pathology results</2> are considered concordant. Recommend <3>breast surgery referral</3>. Read by:***** ***** *****, MD *****/*****/***** 11:56 AM Result Narrative INDICATION:<4>Left periareolar breast mass</4>. COMPARISON:07/02/2016 REPORT: By discussion and use of the consent form, risks, benefits and alternatives were covered.Signed informed consent was obtained. The <5>left breast</5> was prepped and draped in usual sterile fashion. The <6>hypoechoic mass</6> at the 6:00 periareolar position was again identified sonographically. Buffered <7>1% lidocaine</7> was used for local anesthesia with additional buffered <8>1% lidocaine with epinephrine</8> for deep anesthesia. A nick was made in the skin using <9>11 blade scalpel</9>. Under direct <10>ultrasound guidance</10>, <11>2 14-gauge core biopsy specimens</11> were obtained. Pressure was held for several minutes to achieve <12>hemostasis</12>. The patient tolerated the procedure well with no immediate complication. Discharge instructions were given. PATHOLOGY RESULTS:<13>Malignant</13>. <14>Mixed infiltrating ductal and lobular carcinoma</14>. CASE: *****-*****-***** PATIENT: ***** ***** CLINICAL HISTORY: <15>Ultrasound-guided, 14 GA core needle biopsy</15>. <16>Left breast</16>, two passes at 11:30 AM, r/o <17>CA</17>. SPECIMEN: <18>ULTRASOUND GUIDED LARGE CORE BREAST BIOPSY, LEFT</18> DIAGNOSIS (MICROSCOPIC): <19>BREAST, LEFT, ULTRASOUND GUIDED CORE BIOPSY</19> -- <20>MIXED INFILTRATING DUCTAL AND LOBULAR CARCINOMA</20>; SEE COMMENT COMMENT: The tumor demonstrates a single cell infiltrative growth pattern. An <21>immunoperoxidase stain for e-cadherin</21> was obtained and demonstrates areas of tumor with strong membranous staining, consistent with ductal differentiation, blending with foci of smaller cells ***** staining for e-cadherin, consistent with lobular differentiation. Thus, the <22>biopsy material</22> for this lesion has the appearance of a <23>mixed moderately-differentiated infiltrating ductal carcinoma</23>.
