suspicious mass in the <1>left breast</1>.  <2>10/30/2008</2> the patient underwent a <3>fine-needle aspiration</3> that revealed an <4>invasive ductal carcinoma</4> which was <5>estrogen receptor positive</5>, <6>progesterone receptor negative</6>, and <7>HER-2/neu negative</7>.  <8>12/18/2008</8> the patient underwent a <9>left breast lumpectomy</9> and <10>sentinel lymph node biopsy</10>.  Pathology revealed a <11>1.5 cm grade 3 invasive ductal carcinoma</11> which was <12>estrogen receptor positive</12>, <13>progesterone receptor negative</13>, and <14>HER-2 negative</14> by <15>immunohistochemistry</15>.  One out of the <16>3 sentinel lymph nodes</16> was positive.  In <17>December 2008</17> the patient had a <18>completion axillary lymph node dissection</18> and only <19>2 lymph nodes</19> were seen both negative for cancer.  In <20>January 2009</20>, the patient initiated <21>chemotherapy</21> with <22>Cytoxan</22>, <23>methotrexate</23>, and <24>5-*****</24>.  She also started <25>Lupron</25> at this time.  The patient underwent <26>genetic testing</26> and was negative for <27>BRCA1</27> and <28>2</28>.  <29>08/27/2009</29> the patient underwent a <30>CT scan of the chest abdomen and pelvis</30> revealing enhancing tissue in the <31>right anterior breast</31>, a <32>sclerotic focus</32> in the <33>left sacral ala</33>, and a <34>new sclerotic focus</34> in the <35>right anterior superior acetabulum</35>. A <36>bone scan</36> on <37>09/04/2009</37> revealed no uptake to suggest <38>osseous metastasis</38>.  <39>09/12/2009</39> the patient initiated <40>postoperative radiation</40> to the <41>left breast</41>, <42>supraclavicular</42>, and <43>axillary regions</43> receiving <44>1800 cGY</44> to these regions.  <45>09/20/2009</45>, the patient underwent a <46>core biopsy</46> of a mass in the <47>12:00 position of the right breast</47> which revealed a <48>grade 1 invasive ductal carcinoma</48>.  <49>11/08/2009</49>, the patient underwent a <50>PET CT scan</50> at ***** revealing <51>skin thickening</51> in the <52>right breast</52>, a <53>1.1 cm area of soft tissue enhancement</53> in the <54>right breast</54>, no <55>axillary</55> or <56>internal mammary adenopathy</56>, and no clear evidence of <57>distant metastatic disease</57>.  <58>12/14/2009</58> the patient underwent <59>bilateral mastectomies</59>, <60>bilateral ***** flap reconstruction</60>, and a <61>left completion axillary</61>
