explained to her that she now had clear evidence of invasive <1>breast cancer</1>, but noted that her <2>PET/CT</2> demonstrated no evidence of <3>metastases</3> and therefore she can now be treated with curative intent. I discussed with ***** and her husband in great detail the natural history and treatment of early stage *****-/*****+ <4>IDC</4>. I explained that were she to forego the recommended therapy, I am very concerned that she would develop <5>metastatic disease</5> in the short term, even in the context of her 10 year history of <6>DCIS</6>. We discussed the multimodality approach to <7>breast cancer treatment</7>. The patient understands that her treatment recommendations will include <8>chemotherapy</8>, biological therapies (<9>trastuzumab</9> and <10>pertuzumab</10>), <11>surgery</11>, and <12>radiation</12>. We discussed the advantages of offering <13>chemotherapy</13> in the <14>neoadjuvant setting</14> in order to increase the chances of <15>breast conserving surgery</15>, while concurrently allowing us to assess the response to treatment. I explained that in the context of her high-risk node-negative, ***** positive disease we would typically recommend that she receive either 12 cycles of weekly <16>paclitaxel</16> plus <17>trastuzumab</17>/<18>pertuzumab</18> (<19>THP</19>) followed by 4 cycles of <20>doxorubicin</20> plus <21>cyclophosphamide</21> (<22>AC</22>) every 2 weeks or 6 cycles of every 3 week <23>docetaxel</23>, <24>carboplatin</24>, <25>trastuzumab</25>, and <26>pertuzumab</26> (<27>TCHP</27>). I also explained that either regimen would be followed by every 3 week <28>trastuzumab</28> for a total of one year of biological therapy. We reviewed the potential side effects of both regimens. Given her extensive disease even in the absence of nodal involvement, I recommend that she proceed with an <29>anthracycline-containing regimen</29>, unless she is found to have substantial cardiac dysfunction on <30>TTE</30>. I specifically discussed with ***** the typical and less common side-effects associated with <31>THP</31>/<32>AC</32>, such as: <33>fatigue</33>, <34>nausea</34>, <35>myelosuppression</35>, <36>nail changes</36>, <37>alopecia</37>, <38>diarrhea</38>, and <39>constipation</39>. I stated that
