ner_results: ```plaintext
macrophages, hemosiderin and fibrin, consistent with <1>biopsy site changes</1>. Also noted are fragments of <2>necrotic tissue</2>. Relatively scant <3>carcinoma</3> is sampled, most with smooth round contour while some partially disrupted by the granulation tissue and foamy macrophages. To further characterize the carcinoma, the following <4>immunostains</4> are performed and evaluated on block A1: - *****: Positive around some <5>tumor nests</5>; negative around other tumor nests. - <6>SMM</6>: Positive around some tumor nests; negative around other tumor nests. - *****/6: Positive around some tumor nests; negative around other tumor nests. Negative in <7>tumor cells</7>. Positive *****, SMM and *****/6 around some tumor nests supports the presence of <8>DCIS</8>. Tumor nests with negative *****, SMM and *****/6 are largely associated with and disrupted by granulation tissue, which may impact the staining for <9>myoepithelial markers</9>. Overall, the findings are not diagnostic of invasion in this limited biopsy. Patient's prior biopsy (*****-*****) is reviewed concurrently. Given two biopsies with findings not diagnostic of invasion, we would recommend complete excision for definite evaluation of invasion. Dr. ***** ***** has reviewed this case and agrees with the diagnosis. <10>Immunohistochemical tests</10> for <11>estrogen receptors</11> and <12>progesterone receptors</12> were performed by manual morphometry on block A1. The test for estrogen receptors is positive. There is moderate to strong nuclear staining in ~90% of tumor cells. Internal positive control is present, and external positive control is appropriate. The test for progesterone receptors is positive. There is variable (ranging from weak to strong) nuclear staining in ~60% of tumor cells. Internal positive control is present, and external positive control is appropriate. Methodology Fixative: <13>10% Neutral-buffered formalin</13> Cold ischemia time: < 1 hour Duration of fixation: 6-72 hours <14>ER antibody</14>
``` 
