locoregional therapies to reduce risk of locoregional disease and the use of systemic therapies to primarily reduce risk of systemic disease. Informed her that <1>locoregional breast cancer</1> is curable and that the goal would be to reduced her risk of developing systemic disease, which can potentially be incurable. Based on the currently available information, she seems to have a locoregional disease but needs to be confirmed with <2>staging CT scans</2>, which we have ordered today. Because she has <3>HR+ disease</3>, she will need at least 5 years of <4>hormonal therapy</4>. Data from <5>*****/TEXT trial</5> demonstrated that patient who were premenopausal at their <6>breast cancer diagnosis</6> and recovered ovarian function after completion of <7>chemotherapy</7>, the combination of <8>ovarian suppression</8> and an <9>aromatase inhibitor</9> was superior to <10>tamoxifen</10> in terms of preventing distant recurrence particularly in those under 35. Thus, would favor <11>*****+*****</11>, regardless of <12>chemotherapy</12>. We reviewed possible side effects from <13>*****</13>, including but not limited to <14>urogenital atrophy</14>, <15>vasomotor symptoms</15>, and <16>musculoskeletal/joint stiffness or pain</16>. We also reviewed the potential for acceleration of <17>bone density loss</17> associated with initiation of <18>ovarian suppression</18> plus an <19>aromatase inhibitor</19>. We also discussed the importance of exercise in helping to alleviate this symptoms. We reviewed data from <20>***** trial</20> which evaluated 5,015 patients with <21>stage II/III breast cancer</21>, March 30 involved nodes, and a <22>RS ? 25</22>. Patients were assigned to receive either <23>endocrine therapy</23> or <24>endocrine therapy plus standard chemotherapy</24>. At a median follow-up of 5.1 years, there was a significant association between <25>chemotherapy benefit</25> and <26>menopausal status</26> (P = .004). The absolute benefit in this cohort was 5.2% at 5 years for <27>disease free survival</27>. Although it is a short follow up for <28>HR+ disease</28>, there was also a 1.3% absolute benefit in 5-year <29>overall survival</29> in the premenopausal
``` 
