 containing arm (such as ***** or *****), they can switch back to <1>paclitaxel</1> if not responding or receive it post operatively. In any of the arms, if the patient appears to have progression of her <2>tumor</2> either by physical exam or <3>breast imaging</3>, there is the option of discontinuing the study treatment and either moving on to the <4>AC</4> or switching to <5>paclitaxel</5>.     We reviewed the anticipated side effects and toxicities of <6>chemotherapy</6> including increased risk of <7>infection</7>, <8>hair loss</8>, <9>fatigue</9>, and possibility of <10>ovarian failure</10>. We explained that <11>anthracyclines</11> are associated with a small chance of causing permanent damage to the <12>heart</12> (estimated to be about 1% with 4 cycles of <13>adriamycin</13> and <14>cyclophosphamide</14>) and that ***** are associated with development of <15>neuropathy</15> which is most often transient and rarely permanent.    We also mentioned that given she was <16>ER/PR low positive</16>, we would still recommend 5-10 years of <17>endocrine therapy</17> after <18>surgery</18>. We did not discuss the options in length today, but can do so at a later date.    She asked about whether she could continue to work as an <19>ICU nurse</19> during <20>chemo</20>. We advised her that many patients choose to work during weekly <21>taxol</21>, but it will largely depend on which arm she is randomized to, as some of the experimental arms are likely to be associated with more toxicity. Most patients choose to take off time during <22>AC</22> and then after recovering from <23>chemotherapy</23>.     Plan:  -- ***** order <24>TTE</24>  -- <25>Port placement</25>  -- <26>Chemotherapy teaching session</26>  -- She is interested in screening for <27>ISPY</27>; then will need research <28>core biopsy</28>, research <29>breast MRI</29>, <30>labs</30>. F/u pending *****.   -- F/u results of <31>genetic testing</31> (done locally last Mon)  -- After <32>surgery</32>, she will be considered for post operative <33>radiation</33> and we will recommend 5-10yrs of <34>adjuvant endocrine therapy</34>     RTC in 1-2 weeks in person to start <35>chemo</35>    Patient seen and discussed with <36>breast oncology</36> attending 
 Dr. ***** *****.     ***** *****, MD PGY-5  <37>Hematology/Oncology Fellow</37>    
