ner_results: ```plaintext
interval therapy. Scattered small, faintly hypermetabolic <1>left axillary lymph nodes</1> are most likely benign/reactive. There is no significant hypermetabolic <2>internal mammary</2>, <3>hilar</3>, or <4>mediastinal adenopathy</4>. There is a persistent <5>Port-A-Cath</5> within the <6>left anterior upper thoracic wall</6> (distal catheter tip near *****/right atrial junction). Within the <7>lungs</7>, there are scattered areas of presumed <8>atelectasis</8> or <9>inflammation</9>, particularly in the <10>right thoracic paravertebral region</10> (e.g. on <11>CT images 77-80</11> and <12>79-102</12>), as well as presumed <13>atelectasis</13> or <14>mild inflammation</14> within the <15>lingula</15>. Conversely, a prior <16>hazy/ground-glass opacity</16> within the <17>posterior right lower lobe</17> has diminished or resolved. There are probable tiny, partially-calcified <18>scars/granulomas</18> within the <19>left apex laterally</19> (<20>CT image 76</20>) and at the <21>left posterior lung base</21> (<22>CT image 119</22>), also seen previously. There is mild probable <23>left apical scarring</23> (<24>CT image 71</24>), as well as a suspected tiny (3-4 mm) <25>nodule</25> within the <26>right medial lung apex</26> (<27>CT image 74</27>). There is significant <28>respiratory motion</28>, limiting evaluation for other potential small/tiny <29>lung nodules</29>. However, there are no discretely or intensely hypermetabolic <30>lung nodules</30> or <31>masses</31>. There are no substantial <32>pleural</32> or <33>pericardial effusions</33>. There is suggestion of a small <34>hiatal hernia</34>. The <35>heart</35> appears borderline/mildly enlarged, with mild <36>coronary arterial atherosclerotic calcification</36>. Abdomen and Pelvis: There is physiologic <37>hepatic</37> and <38>splenic metabolic activity</38>, with mildly asymmetric elevation of the <39>right hemidiaphragm</39>. The <40>gallbladder</40> is not well evaluated on this study, with suspected <41>cholelithiasis</41> (better seen previously). There is no significant abnormal <42>adrenal</42> or <43>pancreatic hypermetabolism</43>. There is no evidence of an <44>abdominal aortic aneurysm</44>. There is physiologic <45>GI activity</45>, with relatively prominent,
``` 
