non-discrete proximal colonic activity most likely reflecting normal variation. There is extensive <1>stool</1> within the <2>rectum</2>. There is physiologic urinary radiotracer excretion, with moderate bilateral <3>renal cortical scarring</3>. There is no definitive abnormal <4>uterine hypermetabolism</4>. The lower pelvis is again suboptimally visualized on low-dose <5>CT</5> due to extensive streak artifacts from the patient's right <6>hip prosthesis</6>. Small bilateral <7>inguinal lymph nodes</7> are likely benign/reactive, with no intensely hypermetabolic subdiaphragmatic <8>adenopathy</8>. Musculoskeletal: There is relatively prominent <9>muscle tension/strain</9> in the left <10>subscapular region</10> and adjacent to both <11>hips</11>. There are other scattered areas of presumed subcutaneous <12>inflammation</12> or <13>edema</13>, particularly within the patient's <14>pannus</14> bilaterally, <15>lower back</15>, and <16>right lateral upper thigh</16>. There is also more prominent stranding/inflammation of the subcutaneous fat in the left lateral <17>abdominal</17> and <18>flank regions</18> (e.g. on <19>CT images 134-*****</19>). This is new from prior study, and may reflect interval <20>trauma</20> to this region. There are chronic degenerative changes of the <21>skeleton</21>, with stable mild spinal <22>scoliosis</22>. There is a persistent indwelling right <23>hip prosthesis</23>. There are persistent post-surgical changes within the lower <24>lumbar spine</24> and left <25>humeral head</25>, as well as probable post-surgical changes of the right posterior <26>ilium</26> (e.g. a <27>bone graft donor site</27>). There are probable old/healed <28>rib injuries</28> (e.g. within the right posterior <29>ribcage</29>), as well as probable new traumatic <30>injury/fracture</30> of the left <31>12th rib</31> (SUV <32>3.7</32>). Prior activity near the left posterior <33>acetabulum/ischium</33> has resolved. A prior discrete focus of hypermetabolism within the right inferior <34>L5 vertebra</34> (near the right <35>lumbosacral junction</35>) has also resolved, as has prior hypermetabolism within the left posterior <36>third rib</36> and right posterior.
