ner_results: ```plaintext
is mild <1>atherosclerotic calcification</1> of the <2>carotid arteries</2>, left slightly greater than right. There is no discrete or intense <3>thyroid hypermetabolism</3>, nor intensely hypermetabolic <4>adenopathy</4> within the neck. Thorax: There is persistent subtle irregularity/nodularity of the <5>bilateral breast tissues</5>, similar to prior study. In particular, there is an approximately <6>1.4 cm faintly hypermetabolic density</6> within the <7>right lateral breast</7> on <8>CT images 119-*****</8> with <9>SUV 1.0</9> (previously similar in size with <10>SUV 0.8</10>). There are other very faintly hypermetabolic nodular densities within the both breasts, including an approximately <11>1 cm density</11> within the <12>right superolateral breast</12> (<13>CT image 104</13>, <14>SUV 1.2</14>) and an approximately <15>2 cm elongated density</15> within the <16>left lateral breast</16> (<17>CT images 124-*****</17>, <18>SUV 1.3</18>). These findings also appear relatively stable from prior study. There are no definitive new or intensely hypermetabolic breast lesions on either side. The numerous hypermetabolic <19>right axillary</19> and <20>right subpectoral lymph nodes</20> seen previously has significantly improved with interval therapy. For example, there is a residual <21>2.1 x 1.0 cm right axillary node</21> with an internal <22>biopsy clip/marker</22> on <23>CT image 90</23> with <24>SUV 1.9</24> (previously <25>2.3 x 1.5 cm</25> with <26>SUV 15.1</26>), as well as a residual <27>1.2 x 1.0 cm right lateral subpectoral node</27> on <28>CT images 83-84</28> with <29>SUV 1.8</29> (previously <30>1.8 x 1.6 cm</30> with <31>SUV 9.6</31>). There are other scattered residual <32>right axillary</32> and <33>right subpectoral nodes</33> that have similarly significantly declined in size from prior study, and now demonstrate little (if any) appreciable metabolic activity. These findings are consistent with extensive <34>locoregional nodal metastatic disease</34> that has substantially improved with interval therapy. Scattered small, faintly hypermetabolic <35>left axillary lymph nodes</35> are most likely benign/reactive.
``` 
