ner_results: ```plaintext
<1>hypermetabolic internal mammary, hilar, or mediastinal adenopathy</1>. There is a persistent <2>Port-A-Cath</2> within the left anterior upper thoracic wall (distal catheter tip near *****/right atrial junction). Within the lungs, there are scattered areas of presumed <3>atelectasis</3> or <4>inflammation</4>, particularly in the right thoracic paravertebral region (e.g. on CT images 77-80 and 79-102), as well as presumed atelectasis or mild inflammation within the <5>lingula</5>. Conversely, a prior <6>hazy/ground-glass opacity</6> within the posterior right lower lobe has diminished or resolved. There are probable tiny, partially-calcified <7>scars/granulomas</7> within the left apex laterally (CT image 76) and at the left posterior lung base (CT image 119), also seen previously. There is mild probable left apical <8>scarring</8> (CT image 71), as well as a suspected tiny (3-4 mm) <9>nodule</9> within the right medial lung apex (CT image 74). There is significant respiratory motion, limiting evaluation for other potential small/tiny lung nodules. However, there are no discretely or intensely hypermetabolic lung nodules or masses. There are no substantial <10>pleural or pericardial effusions</10>. There is suggestion of a small <11>hiatal hernia</11>. The heart appears borderline/mildly enlarged, with mild <12>coronary arterial atherosclerotic calcification</12>. Abdomen and Pelvis: There is physiologic <13>hepatic</13> and <14>splenic metabolic activity</14>, with mildly asymmetric elevation of the right <15>hemidiaphragm</15>. The <16>gallbladder</16> is not well evaluated on this study, with suspected <17>cholelithiasis</17> (better seen previously). There is no significant abnormal <18>adrenal</18> or <19>pancreatic hypermetabolism</19>. There is no evidence of an <20>abdominal aortic aneurysm</20>. There is physiologic <21>GI activity</21>, with relatively prominent, non-discrete proximal <22>colonic activity</22> most likely reflecting normal variation. There is extensive <23>stool</23> within the <24>rectum</24>.
``` 
