nodes by <1>CT</1> criteria. Heterogeneous <2>thyroid gland</2> with <3>hypoattenuating nodules</3>. Small <4>left pleural effusion</4> with associated <5>compressive atelectasis</5> and/or <6>consolidation</6>. No suspicious <7>pulmonary nodules</7> or <8>masses</8> identified. No <9>pneumothorax</9>. No <10>cardiomegaly</10> or <11>pericardial effusion</11>. There is a <12>3 vessel left-sided aortic arch</12>. The <13>great vessels</13> are widely patent. Normal caliber <14>thoracic aorta</14> without <15>dissection</15>. No <16>periaortic inflammatory stranding</16> or <17>free fluid</17>. No <18>filling defects</18> within the <19>central pulmonary arteries</19>. Extensive <20>osseous metastatic disease</20> involving the <21>axial/appendicular skeleton</21> with old <22>sternal and bilateral rib pathologic fracture deformities</22> with <23>callus formation</23>. ABDOMEN/PELVIS Numerous <24>hypoattenuating lesions</24> scattered throughout the <25>right and left hepatic lobes</25> (series 3, images 98, 105, 106, 110, 116, 117, 119, 124, 125, 127, 131, 140, and 148), worrisome for <26>metastatic disease</26>, increased in size and in number when compared to study dated 03/10/2020. The index lesions are as follows: 1. <27>Hepatic mass</27> measures 2.3 cm (December 115), previously 1.2 cm. 2. <28>Mass hepatic segment VIII</28> measures 2.1 cm (December 129), previously 1 cm. 3. <29>Mass hepatic segment VIII</29> measures 2.1 cm (December 117), new when compared to recent prior imaging. No <30>intra-/extrahepatic biliary duct dilatation</30>. <31>Decompressed gallbladder</31> without <32>radiopaque stones</32>. The <33>hepatic and portal veins</33> are patent. There is <34>conventional hepatic arterial anatomy</34>. No <35>splenomegaly</35>. Subtle areas of <36>heterogeneity within the mid pancreas</36>, possibly related to <37>contrast bolus timing</37> with underlying <38>mass lesions</38> not excluded. Normal <39>pancreas</39> without <40>duct dilatation</40>, <41>atrophy</41>, or <42>focal mass lesions</42>. The <43>adrenal glands</43> are symmetric bilaterally without <44>nodularity</44> or <45>hyperplasia</45>. The <46>kidneys</46> demonstrate symmetric enhancement without <47>hydronephrosis</47> or <48>radiopaque stones</48>. The <49>urinary bladder</49> is within normal limits. Lobular <50>uterine contours</50>
``` 
