 and ***** negative based on core needle biopsy of an axillary <1>LN</1> in November 2019. She presented with extensive <2>lytic bone lesions</2> at that time. She has since developed <3>metastatic disease</3> to the <4>liver</4> and <5>pachymeninges</5> and possibly to the <6>leptomeninges</6>, although her first <7>CSF cytology</7> was negative for malignant cells. We explained that <8>metastatic breast cancer</8> is not curable, but it is treatable and there are therapies that will extend the quality and duration of life. We reviewed her treatment course to date. She was initially started on <9>letrozole</9>/<10>palbociclib</10> in December 2019, but this was discontinued due to <11>PD</11> to the <12>liver</12> and <13>LN</13> in July 2020. WE assured ***** that this was an appropriate treatment choice as initial therapy of *****+/*****- <14>metastatic disease</14>. Upon progression, she was switched to <15>Xeloda</15> (initially <16>500mg BID</16> inadvertantly, then <17>1000mg BID</17> in mid-September, and <18>1500 mg BID</18> since late September). She recently had <19>brain MRIs</19> on 09/29/20 and again on 11/07/20, which showed diffuse irregular ***** enhancement along the bilateral cerebral convexities and right petroclival region as well as *****'s ***** The more recent scan showed possibly worsening of disease in the <20>internal auditory canal</20>, which would be consistent with her recent right <21>hearing loss</21>. We tried to explain to the patient and family the difference between ***** based/***** disease and <22>leptomeningeal disease</22> and stressed that it can be very difficulty radiographically to distinguish the two. While her first <23>CSF cytology</23> was negative for malignant cells, her radiographic and clinical findings raise suspicion for <24>leptomeningeal disease</24>. Recently, her <25>neurologic symptoms</25> are getting progressively worse, with no improvement in her <26>vision</26> and now <27>auditory symptoms</27>. Despite her initial <28>CSF cytology</28> being negative, we explained that false negatives are not uncommon and we recommend repeat <29>LP</29> with <30>cytology</30> now. We also recommend referral to
