were positive and <1>HER-2/neu</1> was negative. The <2>tumor grade</2> was assessed as <3>2</3>. A long discussion with Ms. ***** about the general approach to the treatment of primary <4>breast cancer</4> with the use of <5>surgery</5> and <6>radiation</6> to remove the <7>tumor</7> from the <8>chest wall</8> and prevent its recurrence locally as well as the use of <9>chemotherapy</9> and <10>hormonal therapy</10> as systemic therapy peak to prevent distant metastatic recurrence. Given the <11>tumor parameters</11> mentioned above I assessed that she had a reasonably high risk of <12>breast cancer recurrence</12> in the range of about <13>40-45%</13>. I discussed the process of risk estimation with her and the various parameters that we use to assess risk she seemed to understand as well. Ms. ***** that it would be very reasonable to pursue a course of <14>hormonal therapy</14> to decrease the risk of metastatic recurrence and that the risk-benefit ratio for <15>hormonal treatment</15> would be favorable. Although I believe there would be some benefit from a course of <16>chemotherapy</16> in this case I believe her age (even though she appears vigorous for her stated age) and her history of <17>Parkinson's disease</17> would make the risk-benefit ratio for a course of <18>chemotherapy</18> less favorable. Since a course of <19>chemotherapy</19> added to a course of <20>hormonal therapy</20> only give her an additional benefit of <21>3-4%</21> I suggested I could not strongly recommend a course of <22>chemotherapy</22> that would be a personal decision will depend on how aggressively she wanted to approach his situation with Ms. ***** told me that she was leaning against the course of <23>chemotherapy</23> and would prefer to receive a course of <24>hormonal therapy</24> alone and I to initiate therapy could support her in that decision. Plan: Ms. ***** will return to ***** ***** medical oncologist Dr. ***** for further discussions and definitive treatment
